#!/bin/sh
# ============================================================
# hotplug service — run script
#
# Everything this system knows about its hardware, it learned once:
# novi-hwdetect walks /sys in the initramfs and again from rc.init,
# and that is a complete answer to "what is in this machine" and no
# answer at all to "what did someone just plug in". A USB WiFi
# dongle, a dock's ethernet, a headset, a memory stick -- all of them
# arrive after the walk is over, and until this service existed
# nothing noticed.
#
# busybox `uevent` is the listener: it binds NETLINK_KOBJECT_UEVENT
# and runs a program per notification with the kernel's variables in
# its environment. It also forces a 128 MB receive buffer, which is
# the property that matters -- events queue in the kernel while the
# handler works, instead of being dropped on a burst.
#
# No notification-fd. Nothing observes this service's readiness (it
# has no state anything reads, unlike `network`, whose recorded
# interface boot convergence compares against), so declaring
# readiness here would be ceremony. It is up when uevent is bound,
# and uevent binds before it reads.
#
# Note what this is NOT: it is not a device-node creator. devtmpfs
# (CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT) makes the nodes, in the kernel, before any
# of this runs. mdev exists in this busybox and is deliberately not
# used -- it would be a second thing creating nodes devtmpfs already
# created, with its own rule file to keep in sync.
# ============================================================
exec uevent /sbin/novi-hotplug
